  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "korean-folk-music-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Korean Folk Music — Dance and Rhythm",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** East Asian Traditions | **Region:** asia-korea | **Tier:** 5\n**Prerequisites:** regions-asia-korea-04-genres-forms\n**Tags:** asia, korea, east-asia, dance, and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "folk dance\n\nKorean Folk Music belongs to the **East Asian Traditions** curriculum. pansori masters\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **folk dance** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-japan",
      "title": "Compared with Japan",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Japanese Traditional Music** (`regions-asia-japan-05-dance`) covers the same lesson slot from the anchor perspective. This lesson stresses: **folk dance**.\n\n| Topic | Japan anchor | Korean Folk Music (this lesson) |\n|-------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Focus | noh, bon odori… | folk dance… |\n\n**Parallel anchor lesson:** `regions-asia-japan-05-dance`\n**Secondary comparison (Ireland):** `regions-celtic-ireland-05-dance`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "folk dance\n\nStudy **korean folk music** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Music of Korea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Korea) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **East Asian Traditions**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **gayageum, piri, voice**. Ensemble layer: **janggu, ensemble**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **pansori, minyo**. Dance context: **folk dance**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **court-folk continuum**. Contemporary scenes: **festivals**. Tune repertoire anchor: **Arirang**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- folk dance\n- Lead instruments: gayageum, piri, voice\n- Ensemble: janggu, ensemble\n- Forms: pansori, minyo\n- Dance: folk dance\n- History: court-folk continuum\n- Scenes: festivals\n- Repertoire: Arirang\n- Compared with Japan: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Korean Folk Music is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: folk dance.\n- Compared with Japan: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: pansori, minyo.\n- Dance: folk dance.\n- Transmission: court-folk continuum."
        }
      ]
    },
